# The duplicate finder, end to end: shortlist from the index, separate on content, then remove
# the extra copies through the ordinary reversible delete and put them back.
#
# The fixture is built so all three outcomes are exercised:
#   - twin.txt        500 bytes, copied  -> identical, and settled by the 64 KB sample alone
#   - photo.jpg       96 KB, copied      -> identical, and only after the full hash
#   - archive.zip     512 KB, copied     -> the same, larger
#   - same-a/b.bin    5,000 bytes each   -> the same LENGTH and different bytes, so never a pair
#
# That last one is the point of the whole shortlist-then-read design, and it is free here because
# 'write' fills every file with its own name repeated — two files of one size are never the same
# file unless something really copied one.

tree .
write same-a.bin 5000
write same-b.bin 5000
write twin.txt 500
refresh
assert-count 11

# Real copies, made by TransferExecutor — the same code a drag-and-drop runs, so these are
# byte-for-byte the originals rather than something the test wrote twice.
select photo.jpg, archive.zip, twin.txt
copy to Empty
settle
assert-exists Empty/photo.jpg

# The scan itself. 'duplicates' crawls the folder into fs_entry first: a harness run is unelevated,
# so the MFT pass indexes nothing and there would otherwise be no shortlist to draw from.
duplicates
assert-duplicate-groups 3
assert-duplicate-row photo.jpg
assert-duplicate-row archive.zip
assert-duplicate-row twin.txt

# Same length, different content. If these ever pair up, the sampling pass has stopped reading and
# started trusting the size — which is the one failure that would offer to delete unrelated files.
assert-no-duplicate-row same-a.bin
assert-no-duplicate-row same-b.bin

shot 01-duplicates
dialog duplicates 02-duplicates-window

# Keeping the shallowest copy keeps the original and ticks the one under Empty, which is what makes
# the assertions below name a definite file rather than whichever way a tiebreak fell.
duplicates-keep shallowest
assert-duplicate-selected 3

# Photographed through 'dialog', not 'shot': shot captures the main window, and the ticks are in
# this one.
dialog duplicates 03-duplicates-selected

# The delete goes through PlanDelete/DeleteAsync, so it lands in the Recycle Bin, fills the shared
# undo slot, and Ctrl+Z brings it back. A duplicate finder with its own delete path would pass none
# of the next five lines.
duplicates-remove
assert-missing Empty/photo.jpg
assert-missing Empty/twin.txt
assert-exists photo.jpg
assert-can-undo
undo
assert-exists Empty/photo.jpg

# Nothing in this run may have tried to put another program's window on screen.
assert-not-launched
state
